Well you build your own service provider (which you aren't supposed to do). This is how you use the HubConnectionBuilder:

```C#
var hubConnectionBuilder = new HubConnectionBuilder();
hubConnectionBuilder.WithUrl($"http://{machnie.Address}/signalr/machineHub", (options) =>
{
    options.AccessTokenProvider = () => Task.FromResult(Common.AccessToken.Token);
});
var connection = hubConnectionBuilder.Build();
await connection.DisposeAsync();
```

I can reproduce this:

```C#
var services = new ServiceCollection();
var sp = services.BuildServiceProvider();
((IDisposable)sp.GetRequiredService<IServiceProvider>()).Dispose();
```

This doesn't dispose the service provider and I'm trying to figure out why.

@davidfowl - if we don't think changing DI is the right call, another option is to not inject the `IServiceProvider` into HubConnection, but instead follow the same pattern that Hosting does and call the ctor directly with the actual `ServiceProvider` instance:

https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/e71a9583b4d6c9bd97edd87cda7f98f232f63530/src/libraries/Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting/src/HostBuilder.cs#L304-L308

Or do you think this pattern of "inject IServiceProvider into a `service` and let the service call Dispose on it" is the right pattern to support?
